%%%%** Section 4.1
\subsection{Overview}

In this section we will conduct a series of in-class exercises.  We will zoom in on exactly how each scheduling method works with a variety of real-time task types.   Our exercises will study the following schedulers (with increasing sophistication):
\begin{enumerate}
  \item Our basic {\tt while(1)} loop:  Non-preemptive Round Robin (RR).
  \item The same loop with synchronization timing loop or timer function: Synchronized Non-premptive RR
  \item Special hacks to the synchronized non-preemptive RR to enable different task periods.
  \item Adding a {\tt sleep(int d)} delay option for tasks: Non-premptive synchronized RR with delays.
  \item Making the scheduler dynamic: Non-premptive synchronized RR with {\tt halt\_me()} function.
  \item Handling I/O requests which take time.
  \item Adding the ability to break into running tasks: {\it Pre-emptive} RR
  \item Allowing for programmer-set priorities among the tasks: Pre-emptive {\it Priority Based}.
\end{enumerate}



\subsubsection*{Diagramming Scheduler Operation}
<*chn>
To study scheduling, print out a ``Scheduler Diagramming Worksheet"
<chn> on page \pageref{SchedWorksheet}.
This chart plots time going down the page and CPU
processes (tasks) across the page. We can think of the horizontal axis
as the memory space of the computer. We will put a shaded box in a
square (or part of a square) when a certain task is running on the CPU.
For example, when the scheduler is running, we would shade under the
``OS'' column. We might also have three tasks labeled A, B, C. We would
shade their columns when they are running. Remember, {\em only one column
may be shaded in each row}.

Assume that each line represents 333$\mu$s.   Assume that there is
a system clock which generates timer events every 1,000 $\mu$s.
To indicate these, make a mark at the left edge of every third
horizontal line.

Relax a bit about exactness or accuracy when doing this kind of diagramming.
It would be impossible to make an {\it exact} diagram without full access to
and understanding of the scheduler's code.   The main purpose of these exercises
is to understand key features and attributes of various schedulers as we
generate various scenarios and ask ``What would the scheduler do next?".
The exact answer will depend very specifically on how the scheduler is
implemented,  but we
can learn better how to think about schedulers by encountering these situations.

<*s>

Use on-line PDF scheduler worksheet.

Each Line = 333 $\mu$sec.

Assume a system interrupt every 1,000 $\mu$sec.
Mark interrupts at left edge of every third line.
Each time scheduler services the interrupt it requires 100$\mu$sec (approximately 1/3 of a line). But assume other operations by the scheduler take zero time.

